+This page develops exact capacity results for a few carefully specified multiuser channel models and two feedback/source-channel inner bounds. All alphabets and message sets are finite for the discrete channels; the Gaussian channels are real, memoryless, have independent $N(0,N)$ noise with $N>0$, and use expected average block-power constraints. Rates and entropies use natural logarithms and nats per use. For channel-coding results, error means average joint message error; for the correlated-source code, it means average joint source-block error. Capacity regions are closures of strictly achievable rates.
 
 The independent-private-message, no-feedback DM-MAC region uses conditional product inputs given a public finite time-sharing variable. The Gaussian MAC proof is separate and handles continuous densities and power constraints directly. A physically degraded broadcast channel is fixed by the factorization $W_1(y_1|x)W_2(y_2|y_1)$, with receiver 1 stronger. Perfect output feedback does not enlarge point-to-point finite-DMC capacity, but the two-user AWGN-MAC feedback region is enlarged and exactly characterized by the Ozarow correlation parameter. The Cover–Leung expression is only an inner bound for general DM-MAC feedback. The Cover–El Gamal–Salehi condition is only sufficient for transmitting correlated IID sources; no general network capacity formula is claimed.
 
